## 🔬 THE SCIENCE OF STRENGTH TRAINING

### What Is Strength Training?

Strength training uses low repetition schemes with heavy loads (1 to 5 repetitions per set with 80% to 100% of 1-repetition maximum) to optimize strength increases

**Strength vs Hypertrophy:**
- Strength: 1-5 reps at 85-100% of 1RM with 3-5 minute rest periods
- Hypertrophy: 6-12 reps at 75-85% of 1RM with 60-90 second rest

### Muscle Density Explained

**Denser muscles** means:
- Increased myofibrillar density (more contractile proteins per muscle fiber)
- Enhanced neural efficiency (better muscle fiber recruitment)
- Stronger muscles WITHOUT necessarily adding significant size
- Strength training makes muscles produce more force without the same size gains as hypertrophy

### Neural Adaptations

Study showed weight training strengthened the nerve-muscle connection before muscle growth - neural adaptations come first

**What happens:**
1. Brain learns to recruit MORE muscle fibers simultaneously
2. Motor units fire more efficiently and forcefully
3. You get STRONGER without getting BIGGER (initially)
4. Strength training enhances neuromuscular adaptations that facilitate force production

### Progressive Overload for Strength

Strength gains come from gradually lifting more weight while maintaining proper form - add weight to the bar, not sets or reps

**Key Principle:** Linear progression
- Add 2.5-5 lbs each week (upper body)
- Add 5-10 lbs each week (lower body)
- When you can complete all sets × reps with good form, add weight

